      "category": "bug",
      "rule_id": "proofline:mutable-default-argument",
      "cwe_id": null,
      "title": "Mutable default argument retains state across calls",
      "explanation": "In Python, default parameter values are evaluated once when the function definition is executed. Using `items=[]` causes all calls to `collect` that rely on the default argument to share and mutate the same list object across function invocations.",
      "user_impact": "Subsequent calls to `collect(item)` without providing the `items` argument will unexpectedly return a list containing items accumulated from previous invocations rather than a fresh single-element list.",
      "severity": "high",
      "primary_location": {
        "path": "src/module.py",
        "start_line": 1,
        "end_line": 1,
        "side": "head",
        "commit_sha": "07192bb14d8e5e0df2feb7367dcdb32218bdcb41",
        "snippet": "def collect(item, items=[]):"
      },
